-\begin{center}\fbox{\parbox{0.92\textwidth}{\small\textbf{Stage 1 working draft, not a completed empirical paper.} The source audit and tested computational kernels reported below have been implemented. Physical-stock trends, conversion associations, diagnostic performance and divergence prevalence have not yet been estimated. Author verification and approval are required.}}
-\end{center}
 \begin{abstract}
-Biological-supply accounts and physical ecological assets describe different quantities. We develop a forest-led empirical study asking whether changes in national biocapacity accounts agree with independently estimated forest extent and above-ground woody biomass on matched boundaries and intervals. The design retains concordant, divergent and unresolved outcomes, distinguishes conversion from other disturbances, and evaluates spatially offsetting local net changes without treating them as gross biological flows. This implementation draft reports the first data-access and eligibility stage, not ecological trend results. A frozen national workbook contains \NationalRows\ country-year records across \CountryIDs\ identifiers; \AccountPass\ identifiers pass the account-only 2015--2023 preflight. All eight deliberately selected pilot candidates pass that limited screen, but their physical support and validation gates remain pending. Small version-frozen biomass files confirm access and expose consequential interpretation issues: nominal spatial resolution is not equal-area support, aggregated density is per grid-cell rather than forest area, and change-quality labels derived from the same endpoint maps are not independent validation truth. Tested area, decomposition and cancellation kernels establish computational identities but do not establish measurement validity. Subsequent stages must resolve boundaries, coverage, change uncertainty, calibration and transition validation before an empirical account--asset conclusion is warranted.
+Biocapacity accounts and physical ecological assets describe distinct quantities, so agreement between their changes depends on observation boundaries, measurement definitions and uncertainty. This study specifies a forest-led design for comparing national biocapacity with forest extent and above-ground woody biomass, and assesses the suitability of available account and biomass products. The national account workbook contains \NationalRows\ records across \CountryIDs\ country and territory identifiers; \AccountPass\ identifiers satisfy publication, completeness and numerical-consistency criteria for 2015--2023. All eight countries selected for methodological evaluation satisfy these account criteria. Physical comparability requires separate assessment of territorial coverage, forest definitions, missing observations and change uncertainty. Examination of biomass product metadata and methods identifies three consequential distinctions: geographic cells are not equal-area observations, aggregated biomass density is expressed per grid-cell rather than forest area, and change-quality classifications are derived from the same endpoint estimates they assess. The design separates extent and density contributions, quantifies spatially offsetting local net changes, and requires reporting comparisons on identical observational support. The results concern account availability and measurement compatibility; forest-stock trends, account--asset divergence and conversion associations are not estimated. These distinctions define the evidence required for a physical interpretation of changes in biological-supply accounts.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Introduction}
-A biocapacity account normalizes biological production-related quantities into global hectares. A physical biomass stock, in contrast, measures a specified mass over a defined support. Neither is an automatic measure of the other. The distinction between stocks and flows in Ecological Footprint interpretation has already been developed in prior work \citep{Mancini2017}; the present study does not claim to discover it.
+Biocapacity is expressed in global hectares, combining physical area with productivity-based factors. Above-ground biomass is a mass of specified living woody material within a geographic support. These quantities are related through ecological production but are not interchangeable measures of ecosystem assets. The distinction between natural-capital stocks and accounting flows has an established basis in the Ecological Footprint literature \citep{Mancini2017}. A quantitative assessment requires separate observation operators and an explicit treatment of their domains.
 
-The theoretical ECOMOD companion demonstrates conditional mechanisms through which conversion to a more annually productive land class can raise a model account while capital-land area falls. Its C13 revision quantifies static identification limits and within-model contribution budgets. Those calculations do not identify physical assets in national accounts or establish an empirical conversion mechanism. A follow-up therefore needs independently estimated physical changes and independently validated land transitions, rather than another algebraic residual or uncalibrated ecological index.
+Composition-dependent land-use models show that transfers towards a land class with a higher annual contribution per hectare can increase a model account while reducing another land class's area \citep{Abaee2026}. Such a mechanism motivates an empirical comparison but does not determine the sign of an observed forest-stock trend. Existing European forest-condition accounting also demonstrates the importance of spatially heterogeneous changes within an aggregate \citep{Maes2023}. Forest condition, extent, biomass and regeneration remain distinct ecological attributes.
 
-Spatial compensation is also not a new concept. European forest-condition accounting provides a closely related example in which an improving aggregate can coexist with declining condition in part of the domain \citep{Maes2023}. Forest condition, biomass, extent and regeneration remain distinct outcomes. The prospective contribution here is a reproducible, boundary-matched comparison of account signals and specified asset changes, with explicit uncertainty, coverage and incremental-information tests. It is not a prior claim of global decline, ecological safety, causality, or first-ever novelty.
+The objectives are to define a boundary-matched account--asset comparison, assess the availability and interpretation of its principal inputs, and specify estimands for extent, density and spatial compensation. The focus is a forest--cropland comparison. Classification of conversion requires observations that distinguish forest-to-cropland transitions from harvest, fire, plantation dynamics and regrowth. The present analysis evaluates data suitability rather than estimating these physical outcomes.
 
-\section{Questions and staged design}
-The primary question is whether national account changes and physical forest changes agree on the same country--interval support. Secondary questions concern spatially offsetting local net changes, associations with independently observed forest-to-cropland conversion, and whether additional reporting information improves a declared diagnostic at comparable error and coverage. The sample will not be restricted to increasing-account countries.
+\section{Materials and methods}
+\subsection{Sources and observational domains}
+The account source is the National Ecological Footprint and Biocapacity Accounts, Edition 2026, results and metadata version 1.0 \citep{Lo2026}. The primary comparison interval is 2015--2023. Absolute total biocapacity is the principal account measure. Terrestrial, excluding-built-up and forest-component accounts, together with separately labelled per-person values, define alternative account domains. The account's own area fields belong to its input lineage and are not independent physical validation measurements.
 
-The primary candidate interval is 2015--2023. A 2010--2020 sensitivity is conditional on the documented cross-sensor change limitations. The account vintage is NEFBA Edition 2026, results and metadata v1.0 \citep{Lo2026}. The physical source under examination is ESA CCI Biomass v7.0 \citep{SantoroCartus2026}. Earlier and later product releases will not be mixed to manufacture an observational time series.
+The biomass source is ESA Climate Change Initiative Biomass version 7.0 \citep{SantoroCartus2026}. The product provides above-ground woody biomass density estimates for 2005--2012 and 2015--2024. Metadata and coordinate inspection used the nominal 50 km aggregate and the 2010--2020 change product. Scientific interpretation additionally used the Product User Guide and the published change-processing implementation \citep{PUG2026}. Biomass pixel values and national biomass changes were not analysed. The 2010--2020 example describes product structure; it is not a change-validation dataset for the primary interval.
 
-The initial pilot candidates are Brazil, Indonesia, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Cameroon, Canada, Finland, France and Australia. They represent deliberate logistical and environmental variety, not a probability sample or an outcome-selected set of masking cases. Territorial definitions, overseas areas, source masks, valid observation support, forest definitions, and availability of independent validation remain gates. The accessible account population is not automatically the final empirical study population.
+Eight countries were selected for evaluation of geographic and measurement compatibility: Brazil, Indonesia, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Cameroon, Canada, Finland, France and Australia. This purposive selection provides environmental and logistical contrasts rather than a probability sample. Country-level account availability and physical observation compatibility are assessed separately. The full set of account identifiers is retained in the availability analysis, irrespective of any expected direction of ecological change.
 
